    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate, **Vinnie Pasquantino** (76.21) and **Corbin Carroll** (73.35) headline the Ghost Ride the WHIP lineup, representing our highest ceiling plays against favorable matchups. **Vinnie Pasquantino** benefits from a massive +18% bump via a soft SP_xERA of 4.73, and his ability to maximize the platoon advantage against Arrighetti makes him our primary anchor. **Corbin Carroll** remains an essential start; despite a slight park penalty, his elite speed profile and strong matchup against Lambert keep him in the elite tier. We are also capitalizing on **Mickey Moniak** (73.14), whose +14% platoon adjustment and favorable park factors make him a high-leverage start, particularly given his 58% stolen base potential against a pitcher prone to allowing runners to advance. **James Wood** (67.15) rounds out our priority targets; assuming his position at leadoff, his elite 0.417 xwOBA and the 85% SB success rate against Wrobleski\u2019s slow delivery create a high-floor, high-upside profile that justifies the risk of his pending lineup status.\n\nThe most difficult bench decision involved **Josh Naylor** (55.62), who narrowly missed the cut behind **Austin Riley** (57.08). While **Josh Naylor** provides consistent value, **Austin Riley** earned the nod primarily due to a significant BvP historical edge (63 PAs), which provided the necessary +8% boost to overcome his low batting order placement. Conversely, we are avoiding **Max Muncy** (41.93) and **Dalton Rushing** (39.54) as they failed to clear the efficiency threshold; **Max Muncy** suffers from a negative SP skill adjustment, while **Dalton Rushing** is hampered by a poor batting order projection and a restrictive platoon disadvantage. We continue to monitor **James Wood** and **Dalton Rushing** for lineup confirmation, but the current configuration maximizes our output in stolen bases and run production while maintaining strict adherence to our daily efficiency constraints.",
